.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt TIMERFD 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm timerfd_create ,
.Nm timerfd_settime ,
.Nm timerfd_gettime
.Nd timers read through a descriptor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/timerfd.h
.Ft int
.Fn timerfd_create "int clockid" "int flags"
.Ft int
.Fn timerfd_settime "int fd" "int flags" "const struct itimerspec *new" "struct itimerspec *old"
.Ft int
.Fn timerfd_gettime "int fd" "struct itimerspec *cur"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn timerfd_create ,
number 322 on i386 and 283 on x86-64, makes a timer on the clock
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME ,
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC
or
.Dv CLOCK_BOOTTIME ,
disarmed, and returns a descriptor for it;
.Fa flags
may be
.Dv TFD_CLOEXEC
and
.Dv TFD_NONBLOCK .
.Pp
.Fn timerfd_settime ,
number 325 on i386 and 286 on x86-64, arms the timer to expire first after
.Fa new Ns -> Ns Fa it_value ,
or at that time on its clock with
.Dv TFD_TIMER_ABSTIME ,
and then every
.Fa it_interval ,
or disarms it when
.Fa it_value
is zero; the setting it replaces goes to
.Fa old
when that is not NULL.
An absolute time already past expires at once, by the interrupt between
ticks below, or at the next tick where there is none.
.Dv TFD_TIMER_CANCEL_ON_SET ,
with
.Dv TFD_TIMER_ABSTIME
on a timer of
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME ,
makes the timer canceled at each setting of the clock, until a setting of
the timer without them, though not at the offsets of
.Fn adjtimex
that Linux slews,
.Dv ADJ_OFFSET
and
.Dv ADJ_OFFSET_SINGLESHOT :
its next read fails with
.Er ECANCELED ,
and its next
.Fn timerfd_settime
that arms it arms it and fails with
.Er ECANCELED ,
without writing
.Fa old .
.Fn timerfd_gettime ,
number 326 on i386 and 287 on x86-64, reports the time to the next expiration and the interval:
a disarmed timer has no time left and keeps the interval it was last set
with, as in Linux.
The i386 64-bit time forms are numbers 411 and 410; x86-64 has none, since
its times are 64 bits already.
.Pp
A
.Xr read 2
of eight bytes returns the number of expirations since the last read or
setting, and waits while there are none, or fails with
.Er EAGAIN
for a nonblocking descriptor.
A read of a periodic timer whose counted expiration a setting of the
clock back, or an offset of
.Fn adjtimex
back, has taken away, as below, returns 0, and does not wait, or fails
with
.Er EAGAIN
for a nonblocking descriptor, as in Linux.
The count is taken before it is copied to the caller, as in Linux: an
expiration during the copy is kept for the next read, and a read whose
buffer cannot be written fails with
.Er EFAULT
and loses the count it took.
A setting of the clock makes a canceled timer readable.
A read of it drops its count and fails with
.Er ECANCELED ,
at once when the descriptor is nonblocking and otherwise once the timer
has expired or the clock was set again since a setting of the timer;
a periodic timer goes on, unless it expired and no read,
.Fn timerfd_settime
or
.Fn timerfd_gettime
came since, when it stays disarmed until it is set again, as in Linux.
.Xr poll 2
and
.Xr epoll 2
report the descriptor readable while there are expirations to read or the
clock was set since the last read or setting of a canceled timer.
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /fd
names it
.Dq anon_inode:[timerfd] .
.Pp
A timer keeps its time and interval to the nanosecond, as Linux does, and
.Fn timerfd_gettime
reports both so.
A timer expires when its clock reaches its time, as Linux's
high-resolution timers expire, never earlier: the kernel arms its local
APIC timer to interrupt at that time between its 1 ms ticks.
Where that timer is not the tick, or before a reference counter keeps the
clock, a timer expires at the first tick at or after its time, measured
from the clock's place within the tick in progress, and is late by under a
tick.
A periodic timer counts its expirations from the time and interval it was
given: a read returns every interval that has ended since the time, so one
armed for an absolute time long past counts each interval since that time.
As in Linux, a periodic timer stops at an expiration until a read,
.Fn timerfd_gettime
or a setting, which moves its time on past the clock as it reads then and
counts the intervals passed; after a setting of the clock back before the
expiration, that takes the expiration back, and the timer is due again at
the time it stopped at.
.Dv ADJ_OFFSET
and
.Dv ADJ_OFFSET_SINGLESHOT
of
.Fn adjtimex
take it back too when the offset is negative, unlike Linux, since this
kernel steps the clock by such an offset where Linux slews it and its clock
never goes back.
A relative timer of
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME
runs by
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC ,
as Linux's does, so setting the clock does not move it; an absolute one
moves with the clock, as in Linux: a setting past its time expires it at
once, by the interrupt between ticks, or at the next tick where there is
none, and a setting back keeps it waiting until the clock reaches its time
again.
A relative time or an interval of any length up to 4294967295 seconds,
about 136 years, is kept whole; a longer one is taken as that many seconds,
as Linux takes a time past its own limit of about 292 years.
.Pp
On x86-64 the timerfd calls take itimerspecs of 64-bit fields, and there
are no time64 forms.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn timerfd_create
returns the descriptor, and the others 0; each returns -1 with
.Va errno
set on failure.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width ECANCELED
.It Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not an open descriptor.
.It Er ECANCELED
The real-time clock was set while the timer was set with
.Dv TFD_TIMER_CANCEL_ON_SET .
.It Er EFAULT
A
.Vt struct itimerspec
is not in the caller's memory.
.It Er EINVAL
The clock or a flag is not one of those above;
.Fa fd
is not a timer; a nanosecond field is outside 0 to 999999999, or a time
is negative; or a read was shorter than eight bytes, a read of zero bytes
among them.
.It Er EMFILE
The process has no free descriptor below its limit.
.It Er ENOMEM
The kernel had no memory for the timer, a larger descriptor table or an
open file description.
.It Er EPERM
The clock is
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME_ALARM
or
.Dv CLOCK_BOOTTIME_ALARM ,
which need a privilege this kernel does not grant.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr clock_gettime 2 ,
.Xr epoll 2 ,
.Xr poll 2
